        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "e669a12ae1c27719893d3c99f6bfc96e01c85dde",
            "parents": [
                "54406f74d01d5045345e3c6cbe5fb9cae1518a53"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/89/89489/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Samuel Cook",
                "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                "username": "sblcook"
            },
            "createdOn": 1776808266,
            "author": {
                "name": "Samuel Cook",
                "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                "username": "sblcook"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/issues/283 is closed, we think it should be reopened ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe it was closed due to being unable to reproduce, but I was able to reproduce it. I could post on the issue the steps to reproduce."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Janet Adkins",
                        "email": "janeta@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "jeaaustx"
                    },
                    "message": "Is this a general problem with all models using entity-manager or isolated to this particular model?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe it is general. Entity-manager never registers OperationalStatus on fan inventory objects. Any board whose fan inventory comes from entity-manager would hit this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 209,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "So this doesn't work because the path is like \n/xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/chassis/fan0 ?\n\nAnd the service is entity manager?  So we instead want to look at the sensor (not inventory) for the health of the sensor?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 209,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, exactly. The fan inventory object is owned by entity-manager, which registers xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.Fan on it but not xyz.openbmc_project.State.Decorator.OperationalStatus. Functional for a fan is owned by dbus-sensors FanSensor on the tach sensor object at /xyz/openbmc_project/sensors/fan_tach/<name>, where the read state machine flips Functional to false after the configured number of consecutive read failures (default 5). On EBADR we fall over to that source of truth."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 210,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Janet Adkins",
                        "email": "janeta@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "jeaaustx"
                    },
                    "message": "The PDI for this property indicates if this property is implemented it will indicate the state of the resource.[1] I did not find any documentation indicating the property could be implemented by a different object that should be checked instead. Is this documented somewhere?\n\n[1] https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/blob/a12cc29365efc814fab77677d101f5cfa51d892b/yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/Decorator/OperationalStatus.interface.yaml#L2"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 210,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "You're right that the OperationalStatus PDI itself doesn't describe reading the property from a different object. The piece I'm leaning on is sensor-architecture.md \"Association Type #2\", which says the inventory\u2192sensors association lets a consumer get the low-level hardware item's Functional state from its sensors. That's the path the fallback would walk. I can make that explicit in the commit message.\n\n[1] https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/master/architecture/sensor-architecture.md#association-type-2-linking-a-low-level-hardware-item-to-its-sensors"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 225,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Janet Adkins",
                        "email": "janeta@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "jeaaustx"
                    },
                    "message": "Is the correct implementation here for entity-manager to expose the OperationalStatus interface for the fanPath object instead?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 225,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "I think both are probably useful: bmcweb walking the association keeps it working for any platform where the tach is the real source of truth, and entity-manager mirroring Functional onto the fan inventory helps consumers other than bmcweb."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 230,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Janet Adkins",
                        "email": "janeta@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "jeaaustx"
                    },
                    "message": "We have code that supports other fan types paths than just fan_tach, it seems problematic to assume this path.[1]\n\n[1] https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/bff66f9b658822be3fec688d56efbcef572922c5/redfish-core/include/utils/sensor_utils.hpp#L224"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 230,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "Good point. I'll drop the hardcoded path in the next patchset and use the association walk from the fan's sensors endpoint instead."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 238,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "isn't this a bad assumption? The inventory name matches the sensor name? \nWhat about fans with multiple rotors?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 238,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "We don't already have an association here?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 238,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Janet Adkins",
                        "email": "janeta@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "jeaaustx"
                    },
                    "message": "Our model with multiple rotors the underlying sensors have names derived from the inventory name. They are not an exact match.\n\nThere is an association documented to connect from low level hardware item to its sensors.[1]  The utility function getFanSensorObjects[2] uses that association to return the sensors to a callback function.\n\nIf the entity-manager produced object cannot expose this interface then this association should be used instead to find the sensor reporting the status to give a supported way of finding the sensors. But my first choice would be for the fanPath object itself to expose the interface.\n\n[1] https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/92b89c6ac71f8b94f67f508102704a1cf38e68cb/architecture/sensor-architecture.md#association-type-2-linking-a-low-level-hardware-item-to-its-sensors\n[2] https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/bff66f9b658822be3fec688d56efbcef572922c5/redfish-core/include/utils/fan_utils.hpp#L71"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 238,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "Good points. The plan for the next patchset is to drop the name-match entirely and use the sensors/inventory association via getAssociatedSubTree. For multi-rotor fans it would walk every tach returned, and any Functional=false flips Health to Critical."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 266,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "Too many indents here, needs to be its own function."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/fan.hpp",
                    "line": 266,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Samuel Cook",
                        "email": "samuel.cook@cornelisnetworks.com",
                        "username": "sblcook"
                    },
                    "message": "Will fix in the next patchset with a helper for the per-sensor read so getFanHealth stays flat."
                }
            ],
